\import{base-macros}

\title{Idiomatic Inference for DSLs}
\date{2025-01-30}
\taxon{Reference}
\tag{presentation}
\tag{invited}
\tag{applicative-inference}

\author{brentyorgey}
\meta{venue}{[[psu]] PLV seminar}
\meta{slides}{\pub{PLV-idiomatic-inference.pdf}}
\meta{repo}{https://github.com/byorgey/infer-applicative}

\p{McBride and Paterson's \em{idioms}, or \em{applicative functors}, are a well-known abstraction defining function application in some ambient effectful context.  It would often be useful, especially in the context of designing domain-specific languages, to \em{infer} such "idiomatic'' application, allowing the programmer to use plain function application syntax—but only when doing so is unambiguous.  I will present work in progress characterizing the conditions necessary for this to be possible.}
